  READING

    control gap 0.021 < 0.05, so the threshold
    is above seed noise and the flags mean something.

    4 of 4 substrate A/Bs are load-
    confounded on numpy_sparse. Their DIRECTIONS may well stand;
    their MAGNITUDES are not the model's until re-run on
    numpy_exact. In priority order:

      0.204  norm_init ON vs OFF  [recurrence-needs-norm-init, self-recurrence-stability-window]
      0.123  recurrent vs feed-forward build  [recurrence-is-the-collapse-channel]
      0.122  rounds 6 vs 20 (reinforcement depth)  [reinforcement-tradeoff]
      0.056  beta 0.10 vs 0.00 (the null arm)  [beta-opposes-capacity-and-depth]

    HOW MUCH A FLAG IS WORTH -- the two cases already re-derived on
    numpy_exact, which is the whole calibration set:

      norm_init on/off   flagged 0.204   8.0x -> 1.0x capacity gain
                                         CONCLUSION CHANGED
      rec vs ff          flagged 0.123   ff has no ceiling on both;
                                         rec ceiling 32 -> 16
                                         magnitude moved, CONCLUSION HELD

    So a flag is a SCREEN, not a verdict: 2 flagged, 2 re-derived,
    1 conclusion changed. And 4 of 4 firing means the screen is not
    very selective HERE, at load 0.87 in a nearly-full area, where
    almost any manipulation moves recruitment. Read the ordering,
    not the count. Its sensitivity is UNMEASURED -- there is no known
    case that passed the screen and still changed on exact drive,
    because no unflagged A/B has been re-derived yet. That is the
    experiment that would tell us whether this check can be trusted
    to clear anything, and it has not been run.

    UNTRIAGED, and named so the gap is visible: every parser-level
    A/B -- lesions, fiber gating, mood, ERP contrasts. Each changes
    which neurons win and so is a load-gap candidate.

    These were listed as BLOCKED on the emergent parser running on
    numpy_exact. That was asserted twice and never checked, and it
    is WRONG: the parser constructs, trains and parses on that
    engine, and returns the same categories. It does not request a
    non-default refractory_period, so nothing is rejected.

    Measured while checking (n=1000, k=50, one trained lexicon),
    load per core area, exact vs sparse:
        DET_CORE  0.376 / 0.876     PREP_CORE  0.225 / 0.506
        VERB_CORE 0.305 / 0.670     ADJ_CORE   0.197 / 0.464
        NOUN_CORE 0.230 / 0.530     PRON_CORE  0.154 / 0.338
    The sampler recruits ~2.3x the substrate in EVERY core area, so
    the whole parser sits at a systematically different load than
    the model it is meant to be. Nothing blocks re-deriving the
    parser-level results; it is now just work.
